lete since their components already have code in the repository. Release 0.0 covers magefiles, format, sys, and testutils—all of which already exist. Releases 1.1 through 1.3 for cat, sponge, and du are also done. The real work starts with release 2.0, which needs true, false, yes, basename, and dirname commands that haven't been started yet.\n\nBut here's the constraint: release 2.0 is still pending its spec, and I can't write those specs myself since they need to be human-authored. So even though it's the earliest incomplete release by the priority framework, I'm blocked on moving forward with it until the specification is ready. The generation pipeline targets only cmd/ and pkg/ directories. Documentation/specs are human-authored.\n\nI need to find work within the allowed releases. Looking at what's already implemented: rel00.0 components exist, along with cat, sponge, du, and wc. But rel02.0 (true, false, yes, basename, dirname), rel02.1 (tee), rel02.2 (head, seq), and rel04.1 (ls-extended) don't have code yet—though some specs are still pending. I'm realizing that documentation tasks are actually allowed per the planning constitution, even though the pipeline doesn't generate specs itself. So rel02.0 is blocked until specs are written. For rel04.1 (ls-extended), the spec is complete, and I have the existing ls implementation to work from—I'd need to review the actual PRD requirements to understand what new features need to be added.\n\nLooking at the current ls code, it already handles single and multi-column output, sorting, hidden files, color support, error handling, and SIGPIPE handling. I need to identify what's missing by comparing against the full PRD specification for ls-extended. vs directory argument handling\n\nWhat might ls-extended add? Typically:\n- Long format (-l)\n- Recursive listing (-R)\n- Sort by time (-t), sort by size (-S)\n- Reverse sort (-r)\n- Human-readable sizes (-h)\n- Inode display (-i)\n- Classification indicators (-F, -p)\n- More flags\n\nBut I don't have the PRD content to know exactly. However, the constraints say I should anchor tasks to specific PRD requirement IDs. Without knowing the PRD content, I can't properly create tasks.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. The constraint says \"Do NOT explore the filesystem, read files, or run commands.\" But also \"All project information is already provided in the project_context field above.\"\n\nSo if the PRD content isn't in project_context, I need to work with what I have. Let me look at what's actually available.\n\nLooking at the code status, I see that rel04.1 (ls-extended) has spec_complete but no code started, while cmd/ls already exists. So I'd be modifying the existing ls command to add more features. The problem is without the actual PRD content, I can't identify which specific requirements to target for the task.\n\nBut wait—I just noticed the constraint says I need exactly 1 task. Looking at what's already implemented across releases 00.0, 01.1, 01.2, 01.3, 03.0, and 04.0, I need to figure out what's actually missing and what I can reasonably define as a single task.\n\nI'm realizing I need to focus on what I can actually work with: the existing code structure and the fact that ls-extended is marked as spec_complete but code not started. Even without the full PRD, I can infer common ls extensions and create a focused task around those. Looking at the ls implementation, it covers a solid set of requirements but has gaps in test coverage and doesn't handle the extended features like long format or sorting options that would be needed for ls-extended. The real blocker is that I need specific PRD requirement IDs to anchor any new work, and without those for the rel02.0 utilities or ls-extended, I'm stuck proposing code that violates the constraint.\n\nEven though utilities like true and false are trivial, the pipeline is strict: specs must come before code, and I can't create specs myself since that's human-authored documentation. Looking back at the architecture docs though, I notice that ls-extended is actually covered under the same PRD as core ls (prd008-ls), and the specs are already marked as written for both the core and extended versions in rel02.1, so I might be able to work with that existing PRD coverage rather than being blocked.\n\nWait, I'm seeing a discrepancy in the release numbering—the implementation_status references rel02.0 and rel02.1, but the code_status shows rel04.0 and rel04.1 as the authoritative versions. Since code_status is the source of truth, rel04.1 (ls-extended) is the one with completed specs, and the extended features would build on the core ls implementation by adding flags for long format, recursive listing, sorting options, and other standard ls behaviors.\n\nThe source code itself references specific PRD requirement IDs in the main.go header—prd008-ls covers R1.1 through R7.1—but I don't have access to the actual PRD text to know which requirements remain unimplemented for the extended version. I need to work with what the code tells me about what's already covered versus what's still needed. the constraints:\n1. I can only propose 1 task\n2. I must anchor to PRD requirement IDs\n3. I cannot read files\n4. All project info is in project_context\n5. Source code shows existing implementations\n6. Releases with pending specs can't have code tasks\n7. Documentation is human-authored (DD7)\n\nThe only feasible options seem to be:\na) rel04.1 ls-extended - has specs, needs code, but I don't have PRD content\nb) Some enhancement to existing code that I can identify from the source\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-examine the source code more carefully. Looking at cmd/du/main.go, the header indicates it implements several requirements, though the test coverage is partial. The options struct has scaffolding comments for future flags like -a, -s, -h, -c, -k, -d. But without the full PRD, I can't map those to specific requirement IDs. There's also a discrepancy where the code_status shows du as not started, but the source clearly has a working implementation with tests—so I should trust what the source code actually shows rather than the metadata. I'm noticing that the test directories and file counts are all empty in the code status, which suggests the test documentation hasn't been properly linked to the actual test files even though they exist in the source. Given that most releases already have implementations but rel02.0-02.2 need specs and rel04.1 lacks PRD content, I should focus on rel04.1 (ls-extended) since I can infer the requirements from the architecture notes about extended ls flags.\n\nHowever, the constraint against inventing design decisions means I shouldn't guess at requirements without the actual PRD. A better approach would be to audit the existing implementations for test coverage gaps—looking at what's already been coded versus what's actually being tested to find the most impactful work.\n\nLet me examine the release structure more carefully to understand what's actually planned versus what's been completed. I'm realizing the distinction between the measure phase (which I'm in now, planning tasks) and the stitch phase (which executes them) — the constraint against writing to docs/ applies to the generation pipeline as a whole, but I need to reconsider whether documentation deliverables are even valid for me to propose given that humans own the specification phase. Let me think about what other approaches are actually available here.\n\nFor rel02.0, those trivial utilities don't have a PRD yet since the spec status is pending, so I can't anchor requirements without one. But rel04.1 for ls-extended has a completed spec and references prd008-ls, which I can cite even if I don't have its full content. Looking at the implementation status, I can see the ls command has specs written for both core and extended versions.\n\nThe existing ls implementation already covers a solid set of requirements, and for the extended version I'd expect to add features like long format output with file metadata, recursive directory listing, sorting options by time or size, reverse ordering, human-readable file sizes, and inode display.\n\nSince the PRD is complete and I have the reference (prd008-ls), I can propose a focused task for ls-extended that targets the most impactful features. The long format option (-l) is probably the best starting point since it's what users typically expect from an extended ls command. I'll need to be mindful of the constraint limiting each task to 4 PRD sub-requirements, so I'll select a subset that covers the core functionality rather than trying to implement everything at once. I'm realizing I need to look at what's actually missing from the existing implementations rather than guessing at extended features. The du command has scaffolding comments indicating there are more requirements in its PRD that haven't been implemented yet, so that might be a better place to focus than trying to extend ls.\n\nSince I can only propose one task and rel04.1 (ls-extended) is the only spec_complete release without full code coverage, I should go with that and reference the PRD directly—the stitch agent will read prd008-ls and understand the exact requirements I'm targeting.\n\nLooking at the test coverage though, the tests only verify a handful of requirements while the implementation covers much more ground. Adding more tests wouldn't really constitute \"ls-extended\" work—it's just filling gaps in the existing ls test suite.\n\nLet me step back and check which release in the pipeline actually has incomplete work I can tackle. Going through the sequence, everything up through rel01.3 is done, rel02.0 through rel02.2 are waiting on specs from the human side, rel03.0 is complete, and rel04.0 is also done. That leaves rel04.1 as the first release with both specs and actionable implementation work—the ls-extended features. I'll focus on proposing a task to implement the initial batch of