and R7.1. These gaps represent legitimate, anchored tasks I could propose.\n\nNow I'm examining the cat tests more closely to identify which requirements are actually covered and which ones remain untested, keeping in mind the constraint that each task can address at most 4 PRD sub-requirements. tested with \"b-overrides-n\")\n- TestCatSqueeze: R3.1\n- TestCatShowEnds: R4.3\n\nMissing tests for cat:\n- R3.2 (cross-boundary squeeze - state persistence)\n- R4.1 (non-printing characters with -v)\n- R4.2 (tab and newline exempt from -v)\n- R4.4 (tab as ^I with -T)\n- R4.5 (-A equivalent to -vET)\n- R4.6 (-e equivalent to -vE)\n- R4.7 (-t equivalent to -vT)\n- R4.8 (-u accepted but ignored)\n- R4.9 (processing order)\n- R5.4 (SIGPIPE handling)\n\nThat's 10 missing test areas. With the 4-requirement limit, I should focus on a coherent subset. R4.1, R4.2, R4.4, and R4.5 form a natural group since they all relate to display transformations with -v and -T flags. Since the implementation already exists, this is purely a test task, and I can fit these four sub-requirements within the constraint. I'm realizing the constraint about \"5-7 files\" is actually a target range, not a strict minimum—for a test-only task on a single utility, touching just one file makes sense. But I should reconsider whether this task adds enough value, or if I should look at what the rel04.1 perspective offers instead, since the stitch agent will have access to the full PRD context that I'm missing.\n\nThe safest approach is to anchor the task to requirement IDs I actually know from the source code—R4.1, R4.2, and R4.4 for cat's non-printing display features—rather than guessing at requirements I haven't verified. Let me draft a task around those differential tests. I'm narrowing down to the four most valuable requirements: R4.1 tests non-printing character display with the -v flag, R4.2 validates that tabs and newlines are exempt from that transformation, R4.4 checks that -T displays tabs as ^I, and R3.2 ensures squeeze state persists across file boundaries. These cover the critical untested behaviors.\n\nBut looking more closely at R4.2, the newline exemption is actually handled by the writeLine function rather than displayByte, so the real test here is about tab behavior when -v is used without -T. This is really testing the interaction between those two flags.\n\nSo my test task will cover squeeze state persistence, non-printing character display with -v, tab exemption behavior, and the -T flag's effect on tab display. -T flag and tab display behavior\n- AC5: All new tests pass with `go test ./cmd/cat/...`\n\nNow I'm thinking through the scope more carefully. Each test function will be around 20-30 lines, so four functions gives me roughly 80-120 lines of code. That's a bit lean for the 150-250 line target, so I should expand the test cases within each function. For the non-printing character tests, I need to cover control characters from 0x00 through 0x1F mapping to ^@ through ^_, plus DEL as ^?, and high bytes using the M- notation. For the tab exemption tests with -v, I should verify tabs pass through both with and without the -T flag.\n\nFor the -T flag behavior, I need to confirm tabs become ^I when -T is used alone and also when combined with -v. And for the squeeze functionality across file boundaries, I should test when the first file ends with blank lines and the second begins with them. I'm settling on R3.2, R4.1, R4.2, and R4.4 as my four sub-requirements since they cover the most essential behaviors, even though combination flags like -vET would be interesting to test.\n\nNow I'm checking whether this test-only task fits the planning guidelines—since the implementation already exists, adding tests as a separate task is appropriate. But I'm noticing my requirement count is only 4, which falls short of the 5-8 range mentioned in the guidelines. Adding R4.5 would bring me to 5, but that would exceed the constraint of at most 4 PRD sub-requirements. I need to think through whether I should adjust my scope or if the current approach is justified.\n\nActually, I think I've been conflating two different things—the guidelines talk about task requirements (R1, R2, R3), while the constraint specifically limits PRD sub-requirements (like R1.1, R2.3). These are different levels, so my 4 task requirements might be fine even if they don't hit the 5-8 target.\n\nLet me map this out properly: I can have up to 4 PRD sub-requirements per task, and each one can spawn multiple task requirements that detail different aspects of implementing it. So I'm restructuring to cover R3.2, R4.1, R4.2, and R4.4 from the PRD, with individual task requirements for testing squeeze state persistence, non-printing control handling, and other specific behaviors.\n\nFor the design approach, I'm following the existing test patterns in the codebase—using the fixture creation utilities and differential testing framework that's already established.\n\nFor acceptance criteria, I need to verify that each test case properly exercises its specific transformation: squeeze behavior across file boundaries, control character conversion with -v, high byte handling with -v, tab exemption when -T isn't used, and tab display when -T is active. All tests should pass when run together.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the priority here. The earliest incomplete release needs human input on specs first, and the next one lacks PRD content, so I can't move those forward right now. Filling test gaps for already-implemented features like cat is the most concrete work I can actually do given these constraints. Looking at the test coverage across the tools, cat has the most untested requirements, making it the best candidate to focus on.\n\nLet me verify this is in scope—cat is in rel01.1, which is definitely allowed. And I should confirm the work hasn't already been completed by checking the code status for that release.\n\nLooking at the source code, cat_test.go already exists