iple cases—roughly 28 lines per function with 3 cases, plus doc comments adding another 15 lines total, which brings me to around 149 lines. That's tight against the 150-line limit, especially if the binary content setup gets more elaborate, but it should work. I need to verify one more constraint before finalizing the approach.\n\nSince the project context doesn't include an existing issues list, there's nothing to duplicate, so that constraint is satisfied. I'm leaning toward the cat test task over ls-extended because I can anchor it to specific IDs and have clearer requirements, whereas ls-extended would need PRD details I don't have access to. Let me check the release notes one more time to see if there's a test suite document for the relevant version. I've settled on the cat test task since I have all the requirement IDs clearly defined. Let me structure the task with the specific PRD requirements and reference the existing test patterns and implementation files that the stitch agent will need to work with. Looking at the requirements for non-printing character handling, I need to add tests for control characters with the -v flag that display them in caret notation (^A through ^_), high bytes with M- prefix notation, and verify that tabs are exempt from this transformation.\n\nI'm also setting up the test structure to follow the existing differential pattern, where each test creates fixture files and compares the Go implementation against the reference binary using parallel execution and temporary directories. D3 test files use Go escape sequences to inject specific byte values into fixtures. The acceptance criteria cover squeeze behavior across boundaries, control character display with caret notation, high-byte display with M- prefix, tab handling with and without flags, and ensuring all existing tests pass.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":85799,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"